Modern Phased-Array Radars (PARs) integrate surveillance and tracking functions on a single antenna timeline, introducing a fundamental scheduling challenge: optimizing the finite beam-time budget to balance tracking capacity and accuracy. Although the optimal tracking period is dictated by dynamic target maneuverability, prevailing Radar Resource Management (RRM) paradigms decouple these physical dynamics from the real-time beam scheduler, inevitably causing resource waste or track loss. Tightly integrating maneuver-aware adaptation within a hard real-time (HRT) framework has remained elusive due to technical challenges inherent to radar systems. These include: (i) managing complex, path-dependent tracking dynamics, known as hysteresis, to ensure tracking stability while optimizing resource utilization; (ii) formally verifying transient safety during immediate, zero-delay reconfiguration under non-preemptive constraints; and (iii) navigating the non-monotonic, NP-hard optimization space to efficiently balance capacity and accuracy. In this paper, we propose RT-Radar, the first framework to achieve maneuver-aware period adaptation while guaranteeing HRT safety under non-preemptive scheduling. We make three key contributions. First, we develop a novel asymmetric control policy that proactively manages hysteretic tracking dynamics. Second, we establish a rigorous transient schedulability analysis for non-preemptive EDF that formally guarantees the safety of zero-delay reconfiguration. Third, we design an anomaly-aware optimization strategy, guided by bottleneck load analysis and accelerated by an efficient O(n) incremental calculation, to navigate the non-monotonic space. Evaluation on an industrial-grade high-fidelity PAR simulator shows that RT-Radar significantly outperforms established RRM algorithms, achieving up to 27.3% higher tracking success ratio during overload and reducing average tracking error by up to 60.3% during underload.